.TH CYAML_SERIALIZE_FLOW 3 "2026-09-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cyaml_serialize_flow \- serialize a DOM tree to flow-style YAML
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cyaml.h>

char *cyaml_serialize_flow(cyaml node);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Produce a compact, flow-style YAML representation of the DOM subtree.
Lists are rendered as [a, b, c].  dictionaries are rendered as
{key: value, ...}.  Scalars follow the same quoting rules as
cyaml_serialize().  The output is typically a single line.

A dictionary's own key order in the output is unspecified, exactly
like cyaml_serialize(3)'s own identical note; see that page's own
DESCRIPTION for the full reasoning.

Tag emission follows the same rule as cyaml_serialize(): a custom
tag, or a core-schema tag that does not match the node's own type,
is emitted verbatim as "!<...>"; a core-schema tag that does match
its node's type is never emitted.

Free the returned string with cyaml_serialize_free().

node is subject to the same 500-level nesting limit as cyaml_serialize(3);
see that page's own DESCRIPTION for why this applies even to a tree that
never went through cyaml_parse*(3).

A NULL node serializes as if it were a CYAML_NULL node ("~"); this is
a successful, non-NULL return, not a failure signal (unlike
cyaml_clone(3), which returns NULL for a NULL node).
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Heap-allocated null-terminated string on success, NULL on OOM or if node
is nested more than 500 levels deep.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_serialize (3),
.BR cyaml_serialize_free (3)
